window.AIBEMOCK={"n":6,"title":"Mock Test 6","theme":"Full syllabus","kind":"Full-length paper","blurb":"A final full-length paper in the same pattern. Take it as a dress rehearsal: sit the whole 3 hours 30 minutes at one go.","minutes":210,"neg":0,"pass":{"General / OBC":45,"SC / ST / PwD":40},"asOn":"19 November 2026","q":[{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"An advocate who has been suspended from practice under Section 35(4) of the Advocates Act, 1961 is, during the period of suspension,","o":["debarred from practising in any court or before any authority in India","permitted to practise before the Supreme Court of India alone","permitted to appear in matters where his own interest is involved","debarred from practising only in the court where the misconduct occurred"],"a":0,"e":"Section 35(4) says that, during suspension, the advocate is debarred from practising in any court or before any authority or person in India. The suspension does not remove his name from the roll."},{"s":"Contract, Specific Relief & Sale of Goods","q":"Under Section 32 of the Indian Contract Act, 1872, a contingent contract to do or not to do anything if an uncertain future event happens","o":["can be enforced only if the event does not happen","cannot be enforced by law until the event has happened","is void, since the event is uncertain","can be enforced by law as soon as it is entered into"],"a":1,"e":"A contingent contract is a contract to do or not to do something if some event, collateral to the contract, does or does not happen (Section 31). If the event becomes impossible, the contract becomes void."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under Sunni Muslim law, the minimum period of gestation, for the purpose of legitimacy of a child born after the marriage, is","o":["ten months","three months","six months","nine months"],"a":2,"e":"A child born within six months of the marriage is illegitimate, unless the husband acknowledges it. The maximum period of gestation under the Hanafi school is two years, and under the Shafi'i school four years."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Waging war against the Government of India is punishable under Section 147 of the BNS with","o":["imprisonment for a term of three years, and fine","imprisonment up to seven years, or fine","death, or imprisonment for life, and fine","imprisonment up to ten years, and fine"],"a":2,"e":"Section 147 BNS corresponds to Section 121 IPC. Waging war, attempting to wage war, or abetting the waging of war against the Government of India is punishable with death or imprisonment for life, and fine."},{"s":"Contract, Specific Relief & Sale of Goods","q":"Under Section 185 of the Indian Contract Act, 1872, to create an agency","o":["consideration is necessary","no consideration is necessary","a registered written instrument","the agent to be a major"],"a":1,"e":"An agency may be created without any consideration, unlike a contract in general. An agent must, however, be of the age of majority and of sound mind to be responsible to his principal (Section 184), though a minor may be an agent, with the principal liable."},{"s":"Interpretation of Statutes","q":"The rule of interpretation known as 'noscitur a sociis' means that","o":["a special law prevails over a general law on the same subject","the meaning of a word is to be judged by the company it keeps","the express mention of one thing implies the exclusion of others","general words are limited to things of the same kind as the specific ones"],"a":1,"e":"Under this rule, a word takes its colour from the words with which it is associated, so that doubtful words are understood in the light of their neighbours. It is different from ejusdem generis, which applies where general words follow specific words."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Under Section 73 of the Evidence Act (and its corresponding BSA provision), in order to ascertain whether a signature or writing is that of the person by whom it purports to have been written, the court may","o":["compare it with a signature or writing admitted or proved to be that person's","compare it only with a writing that is made in the court in the presence of the Judge","accept it as genuine if the other side has not raised any objection to it in court","not compare it with any other writing, but must rely wholly on an expert's opinion"],"a":0,"e":"Section 73 permits the court itself to compare the disputed writing with an admitted or proved writing. The court should, as a matter of prudence, be slow to base a finding solely on its own comparison, without the aid of an expert."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"In Olga Tellis v Bombay Municipal Corporation (1985), the Supreme Court held that the right to life under Article 21 includes","o":["the right to strike","the right to form a trade union","the right to livelihood","the right to own property"],"a":2,"e":"The Court held that pavement dwellers could not be evicted without notice, since depriving a person of his means of livelihood would deprive him of his life. A person may be deprived of it only by a just and fair procedure."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under Section 5 of the Contempt of Courts Act, 1971, a fair criticism of the merits of a judicial act, made after its final disposal, is","o":["a contempt only if the Judge so feels","not a contempt of court","a criminal contempt of court","a civil contempt of court"],"a":1,"e":"Section 5 protects fair comment on the merits of a case which has been finally decided, as distinguished from scandalising the court or imputing motives to the Judge. Fair and accurate reports of judicial proceedings are protected under Section 4."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under Section 18 of the Advocates Act, 1961, an advocate who wishes to transfer his name from one State roll to another must apply to","o":["the High Court he leaves","the Chief Justice of the new State","the Central Government","the Bar Council of India"],"a":3,"e":"Section 18 allows any person whose name is on a State roll to apply to the Bar Council of India for the transfer of his name to the roll of another State Bar Council. The BCI directs the transfer, and the name is removed from the former roll."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Under Article 75(1A), inserted by the 91st Amendment, the total number of Ministers, including the Prime Minister, in the Union Council of Ministers shall not exceed","o":["twenty per cent of the total number of members of the Lok Sabha","twenty-five per cent of the total number of members of the Lok Sabha","ten per cent of the total number of members of the Lok Sabha","fifteen per cent of the total number of members of the Lok Sabha"],"a":3,"e":"The 91st Amendment (2003) limited the size of the Council of Ministers to 15% of the strength of the Lok Sabha, and of the State Councils of Ministers to 15% of the Legislative Assembly (subject to a minimum of 12 in a State)."},{"s":"Intellectual Property Law","q":"Which of the following is NOT a requirement for the patentability of an invention under the Patents Act, 1970?","o":["An inventive step","Commercial success in the market","Capability of industrial application","Novelty"],"a":1,"e":"An invention must be new, involve an inventive step, and be capable of industrial application, and must not fall within the excluded subject-matter in Sections 3 and 4. Commercial success is not required, though it may support inventive step."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under Section 262(2) of the CrPC, no sentence of imprisonment exceeding which period may be passed in a case tried summarily?","o":["six months","one year","two years","three months"],"a":3,"e":"A Magistrate trying a case summarily cannot pass a sentence of imprisonment for more than three months. Summary trials are meant for petty offences, such as theft of property under Rs. 2,000 in value, and are quicker."},{"s":"Jurisprudence","q":"The classification of law into eternal law, divine law, natural law and human law is associated with","o":["St. Thomas Aquinas","John Austin","Jeremy Bentham","Hans Kelsen"],"a":0,"e":"Aquinas, a medieval natural law thinker, held that eternal law is God's plan for the universe, divine law is revealed in scripture, natural law is the participation of reason in eternal law, and human law is made by rulers for the common good."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"In the trial of a summons case, the Magistrate, on the appearance of the accused, must","o":["frame a formal charge in writing against the accused, as in a warrant case trial","state the substance of the accusation, without framing a formal charge","commit the case to the Court of Session for trial in accordance with the law","discharge the accused straightaway if the complainant happens to be absent"],"a":1,"e":"Under Section 251 CrPC (now Section 274 BNSS), the Magistrate states the particulars of the offence to the accused and asks whether he pleads guilty. A formal charge is not framed in summons cases."},{"s":"Limitation Law","q":"Under the Limitation Act, 1963, the period of limitation for a suit to recover money lent is","o":["twelve years from the date of the loan","three years from the date of the loan","six years from the date of the loan","one year from the date of the loan"],"a":1,"e":"Article 19 prescribes three years from the date when the loan is made. The period can be extended by an acknowledgment in writing signed before the expiry (Section 18), or by a part payment (Section 19)."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"A decree of judicial separation under Section 10 of the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955","o":["does not dissolve the marriage, but the parties need no longer be bound to cohabit","dissolves the marriage, and the parties are free to marry again after the decree","can be granted only on the petition of the wife against her husband, and not otherwise","bars either party from seeking divorce on any ground of the Act at any later time"],"a":0,"e":"Judicial separation is available on the same grounds as divorce. The marriage subsists, so neither party can remarry, but the obligation to cohabit ceases. The decree may later be rescinded, or be the basis for a divorce after one year of non-resumption of cohabitation."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Order V Rule 20 CPC, where the defendant is avoiding service, the court may order substituted service by","o":["publishing the summons in the Gazette of India alone, and in no newspaper whatsoever","orally informing the neighbours of the defendant of the date fixed for the hearing of suit","sending a copy of the summons to the defendant's counsel who appears in some other case","affixing a copy of the summons in a conspicuous place in the court house and the defendant's last residence"],"a":3,"e":"Substituted service may be effected by affixing a copy in some conspicuous place in the court-house and in the house where the defendant last resided, or by publication in a newspaper, or in any other manner the court thinks fit."},{"s":"Contract, Specific Relief & Sale of Goods","q":"Under Section 30 of the Indian Contract Act, 1872, an agreement by way of wager is","o":["valid, and a suit can be brought to recover the sum that is alleged to have been won","voidable at the option of the person who has lost the wager in question in the case","void, and no suit can be brought to recover anything alleged to be won on such a wager","valid, but only if it has been made in writing and signed by both the parties to it"],"a":2,"e":"A wagering agreement is void, not illegal, so a collateral transaction to it is not tainted. The section does not apply to a contract to give a prize for a horse race for a plate or prize of the value of Rs. 500 or more."},{"s":"Environmental Law","q":"Under the Wild Life (Protection) Act, 1972, the National Board for Wild Life is chaired by","o":["the President of India","the Union Minister of Environment","the Chief Justice of India","the Prime Minister"],"a":3,"e":"Section 5A provides that the National Board for Wild Life is chaired by the Prime Minister, and the Union Minister for Environment is the Vice-Chairperson. The Board advises on the policy for the protection of wild life."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Order XXXIII Rule 1 CPC (Explanation), a person is an 'indigent person' if he is not possessed of sufficient means to pay the prescribed court-fee, or where no fee is prescribed, if he is not entitled to property worth more than","o":["Rs. 1,000","Rs. 10,000","Rs. 500","Rs. 5,000"],"a":0,"e":"In computing the property, the person's necessary wearing apparel and the subject-matter of the suit are excluded. An indigent person may sue without paying court fees, subject to the court's enquiry and permission."},{"s":"Property Law","q":"Under Section 5 of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882, 'transfer of property' means an act by which a living person conveys property to","o":["a living person only, and never to himself together with one or more other persons","a company only, but never to a living person as such, under the Act","one or more other living persons, or himself and one or more other living persons","a person who is yet to be born at the time at which the transfer is made"],"a":2,"e":"The definition requires a living person as the transferor and a living person as the transferee. 'Living person' includes a company or association or body of individuals, whether incorporated or not, but does not include a person unborn at the time of the transfer."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under Section 2(g) of the CrPC, an 'inquiry' means","o":["every investigation that is carried out by the police for the collection of evidence","every inquiry, other than a trial, conducted by a Magistrate or court","every proceeding that is conducted by a Sessions Judge in a pending appeal","every trial that is conducted by a Magistrate or by a court of competent power"],"a":1,"e":"An inquiry is a proceeding which falls short of a trial, such as a preliminary inquiry on a complaint. A trial determines the guilt of the accused, and an investigation is carried out by the police to collect evidence."},{"s":"Tort & Consumer Law","q":"Which of the following is NOT an essential ingredient of the tort of negligence?","o":["A duty of care owed by the defendant to the plaintiff","Damage suffered by the plaintiff as a result of the breach","A malicious intention on the part of the defendant","A breach of that duty by the defendant"],"a":2,"e":"The tort of negligence requires a duty of care, a breach of the duty, and damage caused by the breach. Negligence is a failure to take care, and does not require any intention to cause harm."},{"s":"Public International Law","q":"Which of the following is NOT a permanent member of the United Nations Security Council?","o":["India","China","France","Russia"],"a":0,"e":"The five permanent members, which have a veto, are China, France, Russia, the United Kingdom and the United States. India has been a non-permanent member on several occasions and seeks a permanent seat."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under Section 33 of the Advocates Act, 1961, no person is entitled to practise in any court or before any authority, unless he is","o":["a graduate in law of any recognised university in India","a person who has been appointed as a Public Prosecutor by the State","a person who has the permission of the District Judge to appear","enrolled as an advocate under the Act, subject to exceptions"],"a":3,"e":"Section 33 says that, except as otherwise provided in the Act or any other law, only advocates enrolled under the Act are entitled to practise. Section 32 permits a court to allow a non-advocate to appear in a particular case."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Estoppel under Section 115 of the Evidence Act (and its corresponding BSA provision) is","o":["a rule of substantive law, which creates new rights in property in favour of a person","a rule of criminal law, which governs the offences that are committed by persons","a rule of evidence, which cannot by itself create a cause of action for a suit","a rule of procedure, which governs the manner of filing suits in a civil court"],"a":2,"e":"Estoppel precludes a person who has, by his declaration, act or omission, intentionally caused another to believe a thing to be true and to act on it, from denying its truth. It is a rule of evidence and operates as a shield, not a sword."},{"s":"Property Law","q":"The rule against perpetuity under Section 14 of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882 provides that no transfer can create an interest which is to take effect after the life-time of one or more persons living at the date of the transfer, and","o":["the minority of a person who is in existence at the expiry of that period of lives","a period of one hundred years from the date on which the transfer is made","the death of the last survivor of all of the transferor's heirs and descendants","a period of twenty-one years after the death of the transferee in the transfer"],"a":0,"e":"The perpetuity period is the life or lives in being, plus the minority of the ultimate beneficiary, who must be in existence at the end of the life or lives. A transfer in violation of the rule is void."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under Section 190(1)(c) CrPC (now Section 210(1)(c) of the BNSS), a Magistrate may take cognizance of an offence upon","o":["information from any person other than a police officer, or his own knowledge","an application made by the Public Prosecutor to the Magistrate concerned","a direction given by the High Court in a petition under its inherent power","information received from a police officer alone, in a written police report"],"a":0,"e":"A Magistrate empowered under the Section may take cognizance upon a complaint, a police report, or information from any person other than a police officer, or upon his own knowledge. The last two are known as taking cognizance suo motu."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"The maximum strength of the Lok Sabha prescribed by Article 81 of the Constitution is","o":["552 members","545 members","543 members","550 members"],"a":3,"e":"Article 81 allows up to 530 members chosen from the States and up to 20 from the Union Territories, making 550. After the 104th Amendment ended the nomination of Anglo-Indians, the earlier maximum of 552 no longer applies. The present strength is 543."},{"s":"Alternative Dispute Resolution","q":"Under Section 18 of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996, the parties to an arbitration shall be treated with equality, and each party shall be given","o":["a right to choose the law of the seat of arbitration","a right to appoint a presiding arbitrator alone","a right to a stay of the proceedings for one year","a full and fair opportunity to present his case before the tribunal"],"a":3,"e":"Section 18 embodies the principles of natural justice in arbitral proceedings. The tribunal cannot decide without hearing a party, though the extent of the hearing is for the tribunal to decide."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under Section 9(2) of the Hindu Adoptions and Maintenance Act, 1956, the father can give a child in adoption only with the consent of","o":["the District Judge, who must approve each and every case of adoption of a child","the mother, unless she has renounced the world, ceased to be a Hindu, or is of unsound mind","the Collector, after he has received a report by the Social Welfare Officer concerned","the relatives of the adoptive parents, after a notice has been issued to all of them"],"a":1,"e":"The father's right to give a child in adoption is subject to the mother's consent, unless she is disabled as stated. The mother, if the father is dead or disqualified, may give the child in adoption. Section 9(4) deals with guardians appointed by court."},{"s":"Labour Law","q":"Under Section 3 of the Code on Wages, 2019, an employer shall","o":["pay a higher wage to a man than to a woman for the same work that is performed by them","pay a higher wage to a woman than to a man for the same work that is performed by them","pay wages in kind only for the work that is done by the women who are employed by him","not discriminate on the ground of gender in wages for the same work or work of similar nature"],"a":3,"e":"The Code prohibits discrimination on the ground of gender in wages, recruitment and conditions of service for the same work or work of a similar nature. It replaces the Equal Remuneration Act, 1976."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"In Rev. Stanislaus v State of Madhya Pradesh (1977), the Supreme Court held that the right to 'propagate' religion under Article 25","o":["includes the right to convert persons by any means","does not include the right to convert another person","applies only to citizens who belong to minority religions","does not apply to any religion other than Hinduism"],"a":1,"e":"The Court upheld the Orissa and Madhya Pradesh Freedom of Religion Acts, holding that the right to propagate means the right to transmit or spread one's religion by exposition, but not the right to convert another person. Forcible conversion infringes the freedom of conscience."},{"s":"Labour Law","q":"Under the Code on Wages, 2019, where an employee works in excess of the normal working hours, he is entitled to overtime wages at","o":["one and a half times the normal rate of wages","the normal rate of wages, without any addition","three times the normal rate of wages","not less than twice the normal rate of wages"],"a":3,"e":"Overtime is payable at a rate not less than twice the normal rate of wages, in respect of work in excess of the normal working hours. The Code requires the employer to maintain records of overtime."},{"s":"Intellectual Property Law","q":"Under the Copyright Act, 1957, copyright in a work","o":["arises only on registration of the work with the Registrar of Copyrights at Delhi","arises automatically on creation of the work; registration is not mandatory","arises only after the publication of the work in a newspaper of general circulation","arises only on the payment of a prescribed fee to the Copyright Board of India"],"a":1,"e":"Copyright subsists automatically in an original work on its creation. Registration under Section 45 is optional and serves as prima facie evidence of the particulars entered in the Register."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"'Evidence' under the Indian Evidence Act (and the corresponding BSA provision) means and includes","o":["only the oral statements of the witnesses who are examined before the court in the case","statements by witnesses, and documents including electronic records, produced for the court","only the documents which have been produced before the court by the parties to it","the arguments that are advanced by the counsel for the parties in the course of the trial"],"a":1,"e":"Evidence includes oral evidence, being statements which the court permits or requires to be made by witnesses, and documentary evidence, being documents including electronic records produced for the inspection of the court. The BSA expressly includes statements given electronically."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under Section 13(2)(iii) of the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955, a wife may seek divorce if, after a decree for maintenance in her favour, cohabitation has not been resumed for","o":["six months or upwards","two years or upwards","three years or upwards","one year or upwards"],"a":3,"e":"Section 13(2) gives the wife special grounds: a pre-1955 bigamous marriage, the husband's guilt of rape, sodomy or bestiality, non-resumption of cohabitation for a year after a maintenance decree, and repudiation of a marriage solemnised before she attained fifteen years."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Order IX Rule 3 CPC, if neither party appears when the suit is called on for hearing, the court shall","o":["make an order that the suit be dismissed","adjourn the suit for a period of one year","proceed to hear the suit ex parte against the defendant","decree the suit in favour of the plaintiff"],"a":0,"e":"Where neither party appears, the suit is dismissed. The plaintiff may, however, bring a fresh suit (subject to limitation) or apply under Rule 4 for an order to set aside the dismissal and restore the suit."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"A revision against an order of a Magistrate under Section 397 of the CrPC can be made to","o":["the District Magistrate alone","the Sessions Judge or the High Court","the Supreme Court of India directly","the Chief Judicial Magistrate alone"],"a":1,"e":"Section 397 empowers the High Court or any Sessions Judge to call for and examine the record of any proceeding before an inferior criminal court, to satisfy itself as to the correctness, legality or propriety of any finding, sentence or order."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Order XVIII Rule 1 CPC, the plaintiff has the right to begin, unless","o":["the plaintiff has failed to produce a document on which he relies in the suit","the defendant has filed a counterclaim against the plaintiff in the same suit","the defendant admits the plaintiff's facts and says he is not entitled to relief","the defendant is the State or a public officer who has been sued in his capacity"],"a":2,"e":"The plaintiff begins by leading evidence. The defendant has the right to begin if he admits the facts alleged by the plaintiff and contends that either in point of law or on some additional facts alleged by the defendant the plaintiff is not entitled to any part of the relief."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"A caveat lodged under Section 148A of the CPC remains in force for","o":["ninety days from the date of its filing","one hundred and eighty days from the date of its filing","sixty days from the date of its filing","thirty days from the date of its filing"],"a":0,"e":"A person who anticipates an application in a suit may lodge a caveat so that no order is passed without notice to him. It lasts for 90 days, and may be renewed by lodging a fresh caveat after its expiry."},{"s":"Administrative Law","q":"The doctrine of separation of powers was propounded by","o":["Jean-Jacques Rousseau, in 'The Social Contract'","Albert Venn Dicey, in 'Law of the Constitution'","Montesquieu, in 'The Spirit of the Laws'","Thomas Hobbes, in 'Leviathan'"],"a":2,"e":"Montesquieu argued that the legislative, executive and judicial powers should be separate to prevent tyranny. Dicey is known for the rule of law, and Rousseau for the social contract."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Under Illustration (a) to Section 114 of the Evidence Act (and its corresponding BSA provision), a person found in possession of stolen goods soon after the theft is presumed to be","o":["an innocent purchaser of the goods, unless the contrary is proved by the prosecution","a person who has no connection whatsoever with the theft which has been committed","the owner of the goods concerned, unless the real owner appears to claim the goods","the thief or a receiver who knew the goods were stolen, unless he accounts for possession"],"a":3,"e":"The court may presume that a man who is in possession of stolen goods soon after the theft is either the thief or has received the goods knowing them to be stolen, unless he can account for his possession. It is a presumption of fact."},{"s":"Environmental Law","q":"The Environment (Protection) Act, 1986 was enacted to implement the decisions taken at","o":["the Kyoto Conference that was held in Japan, as the host country, in the year 1997","the Earth Summit that was held at Rio de Janeiro, in Brazil, in the year 1992","the UN Conference on the Human Environment, which was held at Stockholm in the year 1972","the Paris Conference that was held in France, as the host country, in the year 2015"],"a":2,"e":"The Act was passed under Article 253 of the Constitution, which empowers Parliament to legislate to implement international decisions. It followed the Bhopal gas tragedy of 1984, and gave the Central Government wide powers to protect the environment."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Under the BNS, the right of private defence of property does NOT extend to the voluntary causing of death in the case of","o":["an ordinary theft of movable property","robbery committed on the person","mischief by fire committed on a dwelling house","house-breaking by night"],"a":0,"e":"The right of private defence of property extends to causing death in cases of robbery, house-breaking by night, mischief by fire to a dwelling or place of custody of property, and theft, mischief or house-trespass causing reasonable apprehension of death or grievous hurt. It does not extend to ordinary theft."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Under Section 5 of the Evidence Act (and its corresponding BSA provision), evidence may be given in any suit or proceeding of","o":["every fact that any party may wish to prove to the court, whether relevant or not","every fact that is within the personal knowledge of any one of the witnesses","only the facts that are in issue, and not any other fact, whether relevant or not","the existence or non-existence of facts in issue and of other facts declared relevant"],"a":3,"e":"Evidence is confined to facts in issue and relevant facts. Section 5 therefore bars irrelevant evidence. The court decides the admissibility of evidence offered."},{"s":"Property Law","q":"Under Section 55(1)(a) of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882, a seller is bound to disclose to the buyer any material defect in the property or in the seller's title which","o":["the buyer knows, but the seller does not, and which the seller could not discover","the seller knows, but the buyer does not, and which the buyer cannot find out with ordinary care","the seller and the buyer both know about, and which is a defect in the seller's title","the Registrar of documents has noticed at the time of registration of the document"],"a":1,"e":"The duty of disclosure applies to material defects known to the seller and not to the buyer, which the buyer cannot discover with ordinary care. The seller is also bound to produce the title-deeds for the buyer's inspection on request."},{"s":"Property Law","q":"Under Section 10 of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882, a condition that absolutely restrains the transferee from parting with his interest in the property is","o":["void","valid, if in writing","voidable by the transferor","valid for a short time"],"a":0,"e":"A condition absolutely restraining alienation is void, though a partial restraint, such as one limited in time or class of persons, may be valid. An exception exists for a lease, where the condition may be for the lessor's benefit."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Under Section 2(7) of the BNS, a person is said to do a thing 'dishonestly' if he does it with the intention of causing","o":["wrongful gain to one person or wrongful loss to another person","wrongful gain to the person who is doing it, and to no other person whatsoever","any kind of benefit to a person whom the doer happens to favour in some way","wrongful loss to the State or a public body, and to no private person at all"],"a":0,"e":"Dishonesty requires an intention to cause wrongful gain to one person or wrongful loss to another. Wrongful gain or loss means gain or loss by unlawful means of property to which the person gaining or losing is not legally entitled."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"In a Sessions trial, after the accused is convicted, the Judge must hear him on the question of sentence under","o":["Section 236 of the CrPC","Section 235(2) of the CrPC","Section 313 of the CrPC","Section 232 of the CrPC"],"a":1,"e":"Section 235(2) requires the Judge, unless he proceeds under Section 360 (probation), to hear the accused on the question of sentence and then pass the sentence according to law. This is a pre-sentence hearing."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Under Section 303(2) of the BNS, theft is punishable with imprisonment up to","o":["one year, or fine, or both","five years, or fine, or both","three years, or fine, or both","seven years, or fine, or both"],"a":2,"e":"Section 303(2) BNS corresponds to Section 379 IPC. For a second or subsequent conviction, the punishment is imprisonment for not less than one year and up to five years, and fine. Community service is available for first-time petty theft."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Under Section 132 of the Evidence Act (and its corresponding BSA provision), a witness is not excused from answering a relevant question on the ground that it will incriminate him, but","o":["his answer cannot be used to prosecute him, except for the offence of giving false evidence","his answer can be used to prosecute him in any criminal proceeding that is later started","he is not compelled to answer any question which incriminates him in any manner at all","his answer is inadmissible even in a prosecution for perjury for giving false evidence"],"a":0,"e":"The witness is compelled to answer, but he is protected: no such answer subjects him to any arrest or prosecution, or can be proved against him in any criminal proceeding, except a prosecution for giving false evidence by such answer."},{"s":"Contract, Specific Relief & Sale of Goods","q":"Under the Explanation to Section 17 of the Indian Contract Act, 1872, mere silence as to facts likely to affect a person's willingness to enter a contract is","o":["never fraud, whatever the circumstances of the case before the court may happen to be","fraud, if the person who has remained silent has made a gain out of the contract","always fraud, as it amounts to a concealment of the material facts from the other party","not fraud, unless there is a duty to speak or the silence is equivalent to speech"],"a":3,"e":"The general rule is that there is no duty to disclose, but silence is fraud where there is a duty to speak, as in contracts uberrimae fidei such as insurance, or where the silence is, in itself, equivalent to speech."},{"s":"Taxation","q":"The Goods and Services Tax is","o":["a tax on the profits that are earned by a business in a particular financial year","a destination-based tax that is levied on the consumption of goods and services","a tax which is levied only at the stage of manufacture of the goods in the country","an origin-based tax which is levied on the production of goods and not on services"],"a":1,"e":"GST is a multi-stage, destination-based tax levied on the supply of goods or services, with credit for the tax paid at earlier stages, so that the tax is ultimately borne by the final consumer."},{"s":"Jurisprudence","q":"Leon Duguit, a French jurist, based his theory of law on","o":["pure theory of norms","command of sovereign","spirit of the people","social solidarity"],"a":3,"e":"Duguit rejected the idea of sovereignty and subjective rights, and said that the basis of law is social solidarity, or the interdependence of individuals in society. Law is the rule of social interdependence, which each person is bound to perform as a social function."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under Section 4 of the Contempt of Courts Act, 1971, a fair and accurate report of a judicial proceeding is","o":["a criminal contempt of court","a civil contempt of court","not a contempt of court at all","a contempt if the Judge feels so"],"a":2,"e":"Section 4 says that a person is not guilty of contempt for publishing a fair and accurate report of a judicial proceeding or any stage of it. The protection does not extend to proceedings held in camera, which are dealt with in Section 7."},{"s":"Cyber Law","q":"The Information Technology Act, 2000 is based on the model law adopted by the","o":["the World Trade Organization, which adopted it at its Ministerial Conference in 1995","the International Telecommunication Union, which adopted it at Geneva in 1998","the World Intellectual Property Organization, which adopted it at Geneva in 1996","the UN Commission on International Trade Law (UNCITRAL), which adopted it in 1996"],"a":3,"e":"The Act was passed to give legal recognition to electronic commerce and electronic records, in line with the UNCITRAL Model Law on Electronic Commerce, 1996, which the UN General Assembly recommended that States adopt."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Under Article 74(1), as amended by the 44th Amendment, the President, in the exercise of his functions, may","o":["act at his own discretion in all the matters of government, as he thinks fit","refer the advice to the Supreme Court for its binding opinion on the question","reject the advice of the Council of Ministers in all cases, at his own will","require reconsideration once, but must act on the advice given afterwards"],"a":3,"e":"The 42nd Amendment made the advice binding, and the 44th Amendment allowed the President to require reconsideration once. After reconsideration, he must act in accordance with the advice tendered."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Order XX Rule 1 CPC, after the hearing is concluded, the court shall pronounce judgment in open court, either at once or on some future day, within","o":["thirty days from the date of conclusion of the hearing","ninety days from the date of conclusion of the hearing","one hundred and twenty days from the date of conclusion of the hearing","fifteen days from the date of conclusion of the hearing"],"a":0,"e":"If judgment is not pronounced at once, it must be pronounced within 30 days from the conclusion of the hearing. Where it is not practicable, the court must fix a future day, not later than 60 days, and give due notice to the parties."},{"s":"Land Acquisition Law","q":"The Right to Fair Compensation and Transparency in Land Acquisition, Rehabilitation and Resettlement Act, 2013 came into force on","o":["1 January 2013","26 January 2014","1 January 2014","1 April 2014"],"a":2,"e":"The Act received the President's assent in September 2013, and was brought into force on 1 January 2014. The 2015 Ordinances to amend the Act lapsed."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under Section 8 of the Hindu Succession Act, 1956, if a Hindu male dies intestate leaving no Class I heir, his property devolves upon","o":["the cognates alone, before the agnates","the Government, by escheat, without any other heirs","the nearest relative, chosen by the court","the Class II heirs, and then on agnates and cognates"],"a":3,"e":"Under Section 8, the property devolves first on Class I heirs, secondly on Class II heirs, thirdly on agnates, and lastly on cognates. Escheat to the Government arises only if there is no heir at all, under Section 29."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under Section 17 of the Advocates Act, 1961, an advocate may be enrolled on","o":["only one State roll at a time","the roll of the High Court alone","any two State rolls at the same time","every State roll in which he practises"],"a":0,"e":"Section 17(4) provides that no person shall be enrolled as an advocate on more than one State roll at any time. He may transfer his name to another State roll under Section 18."},{"s":"Alternative Dispute Resolution","q":"Under Section 13(2) of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996, a party who intends to challenge an arbitrator must send a written statement of the reasons within","o":["sixty days after becoming aware of the constitution of the tribunal or the ground for challenge","seven days after becoming aware of the constitution of the tribunal or the ground for challenge","fifteen days after becoming aware of the tribunal's constitution or the ground for challenge","thirty days after becoming aware of the constitution of the tribunal or the ground for challenge"],"a":2,"e":"If the challenge is not successful before the tribunal, the tribunal continues the proceedings and makes the award. The party may then challenge it by applying to set aside the award under Section 34."},{"s":"Cyber Law","q":"Under the Information Technology Act, 2000, a 'digital signature' is created by means of","o":["a symmetric key shared by both of the parties","an asymmetric crypto system and a hash function","a password issued by the Government to the user","a scanned image of the signature of a person"],"a":1,"e":"A digital signature is created by the signer using his private key, with the verification by the public key, and a hash function that makes the signature specific to the electronic record. It authenticates the electronic record."},{"s":"Public International Law","q":"Custom as a source of international law requires two elements, namely","o":["judicial decisions, and academic writing","state practice, and a Security Council resolution","treaty practice, and ratification","state practice, and opinio juris"],"a":3,"e":"Customary international law requires a general and consistent state practice (usus), and the belief that the practice is obligatory as a matter of law (opinio juris sive necessitatis), as stated by the ICJ in the North Sea Continental Shelf cases."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Section 132 of the CPC, a woman who, according to the customs and manners of the country, ought not to be compelled to appear in public, is","o":["bound to appear only before a woman Judge","exempt from attending court, in every proceeding at all","exempt from personal appearance in court","bound to appear in court like any other person"],"a":2,"e":"Section 132 exempts such women from personal appearance, but they are not exempt from arrest in execution of a decree in a civil case, other than for money. It reflects a social custom, and has little application now."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Under Section 8 of the Evidence Act (and its corresponding BSA provision), the conduct of any party to a proceeding, influenced by any fact in issue, is","o":["irrelevant, as conduct is not a relevant fact in any case or proceeding before a court","relevant, if the conduct is influenced by, or itself influences, a fact in issue in the case","relevant only if it is proved by the evidence of at least two independent witnesses","relevant only if the party whose conduct is in question is the accused in a criminal case"],"a":1,"e":"Section 8 makes relevant the motive or preparation for a fact in issue, and the conduct of a party or his agent in reference to the proceeding. Statements accompanying or explaining the conduct are also relevant."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Section 24 of the CPC, a suit pending in a subordinate court may be transferred to another competent subordinate court by","o":["the District Court or the High Court","the Registrar of the High Court","the Chief Judicial Magistrate of the district","the Collector of the district"],"a":0,"e":"Under Section 24, the High Court may withdraw or transfer any suit, appeal or other proceeding pending before it or before a court subordinate to it, and the District Court may transfer any suit pending in a subordinate court to another competent subordinate court, on an application by a party or on its own motion."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under the Maintenance and Welfare of Parents and Senior Citizens Act, 2007, a 'senior citizen' means a person who is a citizen of India and has attained the age of","o":["sixty years or above","seventy years or above","sixty-five years or above","fifty-five years or above"],"a":0,"e":"The Act defines a senior citizen as a citizen who has attained 60 years of age or above. A parent, whether or not a senior citizen, may claim maintenance from his children or relatives, as the Act defines 'parent'."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"The evidence of a test identification parade is","o":["substantive evidence, which can by itself sustain a conviction without any other proof","not substantive evidence, but it corroborates the identification that is made in court","inadmissible, as it is held behind the back and in the absence of the accused person","admissible only if it is held by a Judge of the High Court in the presence of counsel"],"a":1,"e":"A test identification parade, conducted under Section 9 of the Evidence Act (identity), only corroborates the identification in court, which is the substantive evidence. The parade is not required by law, but is a rule of prudence."},{"s":"Tort & Consumer Law","q":"Under the Consumer Protection Act, 2019, a person who obtains goods for resale or for any commercial purpose is","o":["a consumer, if the goods cost a lot","a consumer, if a bill was issued","a consumer, if paid in full","not a 'consumer' under the Act"],"a":3,"e":"Section 2(7) excludes a person who obtains goods for resale or for a commercial purpose from the definition of a consumer. It includes a person who buys goods or avails of services for self-employment to earn his livelihood, as an exception."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"In T.K. Rangarajan v Government of Tamil Nadu (2003), the Supreme Court held that Government employees","o":["have a right to strike, if a majority of them vote for it","have no fundamental, legal or moral right to strike","have a fundamental right to strike under Article 19(1)(c)","have a right to strike, but only for a period of three days"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that the right to form associations under Article 19(1)(c) does not include a right to strike. The right to strike is a statutory right, subject to restrictions. The mass dismissal of striking employees was upheld."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Under Section 109(1) of the BNS, an attempt to murder is punishable with imprisonment up to","o":["ten years, and fine","seven years, and fine","five years, and fine","three years, and fine"],"a":0,"e":"Section 109(1) BNS corresponds to Section 307 IPC. If hurt is caused to any person by the act, the offender is liable to imprisonment for life, or the punishment as above."},{"s":"Jurisprudence","q":"The principle of utility, or the 'greatest happiness of the greatest number', is chiefly associated with","o":["Roscoe Pound","Hans Kelsen","Friedrich Carl von Savigny","Jeremy Bentham"],"a":3,"e":"Bentham, the founder of utilitarianism, held that the object of legislation is the greatest happiness of the greatest number, and measured the goodness of laws by their utility. His pupil, John Austin, developed the analytical positivist school."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under Section 3 of the Contempt of Courts Act, 1971, the publication of any matter which interferes with a pending proceeding is NOT a contempt if the publisher","o":["had a reasonable ground to believe that the proceeding was pending","published it with the intention of helping one of the parties","had no reasonable grounds to believe that the proceeding was pending","published it after the proceeding had been listed for hearing"],"a":2,"e":"Section 3 protects 'innocent publication and distribution', where the person had no reasonable grounds for believing that the proceeding was pending at the time. A proceeding is pending, in a civil case, when it is instituted, and in a criminal case, when the charge sheet is filed."},{"s":"Taxation","q":"Article 265 of the Constitution provides that no tax shall be levied or collected except","o":["by approval of the C&AG","by order of the President","by authority of law","by an order of the Cabinet"],"a":2,"e":"Article 265 requires a tax to have the authority of a valid law made by the competent legislature. A tax levied by executive order alone is invalid."},{"s":"Interpretation of Statutes","q":"Under Section 3(42) of the General Clauses Act, 1897, the word 'person' includes","o":["any company, association or body of individuals, whether incorporated or not","only a natural person who is a citizen of India, and not any artificial person","only a natural person who has attained the age of majority under the law of the land","only a company that has been incorporated under a statute of the Parliament or State"],"a":0,"e":"The definition is inclusive, and covers natural as well as artificial persons. It is subject to any contrary intention in the Act in which the word appears."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"While arresting a person, a police officer may cause the death of that person if he resists the arrest, only if he is accused of an offence punishable with","o":["any cognizable offence at all","death or imprisonment for life","imprisonment for ten years or more","imprisonment for seven years or more"],"a":1,"e":"Section 43(3) BNSS (earlier Section 46(3) CrPC) says that the right to arrest does not extend to causing the death of a person who is not accused of an offence punishable with death or imprisonment for life. Force may be used only to the extent needed to effect the arrest."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Section 2(9) of the CPC, a 'judgment' means","o":["the formal expression of any decision of a civil court, not being a decree","the formal expression of an adjudication of the rights of the parties","the statement given by the Judge of the grounds of a decree or order","the order passed by the court on any interlocutory application"],"a":2,"e":"A judgment is the statement of the grounds of a decree or order. A decree is the formal expression of an adjudication that conclusively determines the rights of parties, and an order is the formal expression of any decision that is not a decree."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under Section 3 of the Advocates Act, 1961, a State Bar Council whose electorate exceeds 10,000 advocates consists of how many members?","o":["fifteen members","twenty members","thirty members","twenty-five members"],"a":3,"e":"Section 3(2) fixes the strength at 15 members where the electorate does not exceed 5,000, 20 members where it exceeds 5,000 but not 10,000, and 25 members where it exceeds 10,000, in addition to the ex officio Advocate-General."},{"s":"Alternative Dispute Resolution","q":"Under Section 10 of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996, the number of arbitrators shall not be","o":["an even number","an odd number","more than one","less than three"],"a":0,"e":"The parties are free to determine the number of arbitrators, but the number must not be even. If they fail to determine it, the arbitral tribunal shall consist of a sole arbitrator."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under the Indian Christian Marriage Act, 1872, a marriage solemnised in a church must be solemnised between the hours of","o":["eight in the morning and five in the evening","six in the morning and seven in the evening","six in the evening and six in the morning","nine in the morning and nine in the night"],"a":1,"e":"Section 9 requires solemnisation between 6 a.m. and 7 p.m., in the presence of at least two credible witnesses, besides the person solemnising. The marriage must be solemnised by a clergyman, a minister or a Marriage Registrar."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"A case triable exclusively by the Court of Session is committed to that court by","o":["the police officer who investigated","the High Court of the State","a Magistrate","the district Public Prosecutor"],"a":2,"e":"Under Section 209 CrPC (now Section 232 BNSS), when it appears to a Magistrate, in a case instituted on a police report or a complaint, that the offence is triable exclusively by the Court of Session, he commits the case to it."},{"s":"Administrative Law","q":"In Ram Jawaya Kapur v State of Punjab (1955), the Supreme Court held that the Indian Constitution","o":["recognises a rigid separation of powers, exactly as the Constitution of the United States does","does not recognise the separation of powers in its absolute and rigid form","vests all the three powers of the State in the Parliament of India alone","does not recognise any separation of the functions of the organs of the State at all"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that the functions of the organs of the State are sufficiently differentiated in the Constitution, though the separation is not rigid. The executive may exercise some legislative and judicial functions, but the Constitution does not allow one organ to take over the essential functions of another."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Under the proviso to Article 3, a Bill for the formation of a new State or alteration of the boundaries of a State can be introduced in Parliament only on the recommendation of","o":["the Election Commission","the Supreme Court of India","the Governor of the State","the President"],"a":3,"e":"The Bill must be introduced on the President's recommendation, and the President must refer it to the State Legislature concerned for its views, though Parliament is not bound by those views. Such a law is not an amendment under Article 368."},{"s":"Company Law","q":"Under the Companies Act, 2013, the maximum number of members in a private company, excluding past and present employees who are members, is","o":["two hundred","five hundred","fifty","one hundred"],"a":0,"e":"Section 2(68) restricts the number of members of a private company to 200, apart from the employees. A private company must also have a minimum of two members, and restrict the right to transfer its shares."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Rioting is punishable under Section 191(2) of the BNS with imprisonment up to","o":["three years, or fine, or both","one year, or fine, or both","two years, or fine, or both","five years, or fine, or both"],"a":2,"e":"Section 191 BNS corresponds to Sections 146 and 147 IPC. The offence of rioting is committed when force or violence is used by an unlawful assembly in prosecution of the common object. Rioting armed with a deadly weapon is punishable with up to five years."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"A warrant of arrest issued by a court remains in force","o":["for a period of six months from the date of its issue by the court","until it is cancelled by the court, or executed by the police","only until the next date of hearing fixed in the case before the court","for a period of one year from the date of its issue by the court"],"a":1,"e":"Under Section 70(2) CrPC (now BNSS), a warrant of arrest remains in force until it is cancelled by the court which issued it, or until it is executed. Unlike a summons, it does not lapse on the expiry of a fixed period."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Under Section 103(1) of the BNS, the punishment for murder is","o":["imprisonment for not less than seven years","imprisonment for life only, and fine","death or imprisonment for life, and fine","imprisonment up to ten years, and fine"],"a":2,"e":"Section 103(1) BNS corresponds to Section 302 IPC. The sentence of death is imposed only in the rarest of rare cases, in accordance with Bachan Singh v State of Punjab (1980)."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under Section 13(1)(vii) of the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955, a spouse may seek divorce if the other spouse has not been heard of as being alive for a period of","o":["three years or more","five years or more","two years or more","seven years or more"],"a":3,"e":"The ground is that the respondent has not been heard of as being alive for seven years or more by those who would naturally have heard of him if he had been alive. This is a statutory presumption of civil death."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under Section 144 CrPC (now Section 163 of the BNSS), an order made by a Magistrate in urgent cases of nuisance or apprehended danger shall not remain in force for more than","o":["six months, unless it is extended by the State Government","one month, unless it is extended by the State Government","two months, unless it is extended by the State Government","three months, unless it is extended by the State Government"],"a":2,"e":"The order cannot remain in force beyond two months from its making, but the State Government may extend it, by notification, for up to six months, if needed to prevent danger to human life, health or safety or a riot or an affray."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"In the Berubari Union case (1960), the Supreme Court held that cession of Indian territory to a foreign country requires","o":["a treaty alone, which needs no legislation of any kind at all","a law made by Parliament under Article 3 of the Constitution of India","an executive order made by the President of India in council","an amendment of the Constitution made under Article 368 by Parliament"],"a":3,"e":"The Court held that Article 3 deals only with the internal readjustment of States and cannot be used to cede territory to another country. The 9th Amendment (1960) was passed to implement the agreement, and the 100th Amendment (2015) did so for the Bangladesh land boundary."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Which of the following is NOT a way of abetting the doing of a thing under Section 45 of the BNS?","o":["Instigating a person to do the act","Engaging with other persons in a conspiracy to do the act","Approving of the act after it has been committed","Intentionally aiding the doing of the act by an illegal omission"],"a":2,"e":"Abetment is by instigation, by engaging in a conspiracy for the doing of a thing, if an act or illegal omission takes place in pursuance of it, or by intentionally aiding by any act or illegal omission. Mere approval after the event is not abetment."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Under Article 217(1) of the Constitution, a Judge of a High Court holds office until he attains the age of","o":["sixty-five years","sixty-eight years","sixty-two years","sixty years"],"a":2,"e":"A High Court Judge retires at 62, and a Supreme Court Judge at 65 (Article 124(2)). The age of a High Court Judge is determined by the President after consulting the Chief Justice of India, under Article 217(3)."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Section 38 of the CPC, a decree may be executed","o":["by the court which passed it, or the court to which it is sent for execution","only by the High Court of the State, on a petition made to it by the decree-holder","only by the Collector of the district concerned, acting under a warrant of the court","only by the court which passed it, and by no other court of the country at all"],"a":0,"e":"A decree can be executed by the court that passed it or by the court to which it is sent for execution, under Section 39, if the judgment-debtor resides or has property outside the local limits of the first court."},{"s":"Company Law","q":"Under Section 7(2) of the Companies Act, 2013, a certificate of incorporation issued by the Registrar is","o":["conclusive evidence that the Act's requirements as to registration have been complied with","only prima facie evidence of compliance with the requirements of the Act as to registration","valid only for a period of one year from the date on which it has been issued","not evidence at all of the date on which the company has been incorporated"],"a":0,"e":"The certificate is conclusive evidence of the due registration of the company, and the company comes into existence as a body corporate, with perpetual succession, from the date of incorporation mentioned in it."},{"s":"Labour Law","q":"Under the Industrial Relations Code, 2020, the definition of 'strike' includes the concerted casual leave on a given day by","o":["seventy-five per cent or more workers","fifty per cent or more workers","twenty per cent or more workers","ten per cent or more workers"],"a":1,"e":"The Code's definition of strike covers cessation of work by a body of workers acting in combination, a concerted refusal to continue to work, and concerted casual leave on a given day by 50% or more workers employed in an industry."},{"s":"Land Acquisition Law","q":"The RFCTLARR Act, 2013 replaced the","o":["National Highways Act, 1956","Land Acquisition Act, 1870","Land Acquisition Act, 1894","Requisitioning and Acquisition of Immovable Property Act, 1952"],"a":2,"e":"The 2013 Act repealed the colonial Land Acquisition Act, 1894, though the 1894 Act continues to apply for certain purposes under Section 24, and the other Acts listed in the Fourth Schedule are brought within the compensation and R&R provisions."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"In People's Union for Democratic Rights v Union of India (1982) (the Asiad Workers case), the Supreme Court held that payment of wages below the minimum wage amounts to","o":["forced labour under Article 23","a violation of Article 19(1)(g) alone","a civil wrong, with no constitutional remedy","bonded labour under Article 21A"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held that 'forced labour' under Article 23 includes labour obtained for less than the minimum wage, as the worker is compelled by economic necessity. The right under Article 23 is available against private persons also."}]};
